<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    Provisioning Console test suite, in two halves — split by WHETHER A TEST
    NEEDS A DATABASE, which is the only distinction that changes how you run it.

    Unit needs nothing. It boots the console application but opens no
    connection, so the endpoint logic, the command wiring and the SchemaDiff
    comparison can be pinned on any machine, including a fresh clone.

    Feature needs the live SUPABASE project. Some of it needs Supabase
    specifically — Supavisor's pooling and role caching, `<role>.<project-ref>`
    usernames, CREATE ROLE, the measured lag windows in PoolerLag. The rest
    (SchemaParityTest, SchemaInvariantsTest) is stock Postgres and would run
    against any server, but there is no second server and deliberately so: one
    more database to install, configure and keep on the right version is a worse
    trade than a slower run against the real thing. Tests\TestCase skips the
    whole half when CONSOLE_DB_PASSWORD is absent, so a clean clone stays green.

    THERE IS NO CI. This repo runs no GitHub Actions workflow; `composer verify`
    and `composer verify:console` on a developer machine are the whole gate.
    That is a deliberate choice, not an omission — the local chain is a strict
    superset of what a workflow was running (it adds phpstan, pint, tsc, eslint,
    prettier and the Playwright e2e suite, none of which CI ever ran), and
    pointing a hosted runner at the real project would have meant admin
    credentials in a secret store, unattended writes to production, and leaked
    biz_test_* schemas on every cancelled run.

    `composer verify:console` runs BOTH halves with the "fail on skipped" flag.
    That flag is the honesty guard: Tests\TestCase skips silently, so a
    console/.env missing its password would otherwise produce a green run that
    executed nothing. That exact shape is why the parity guard sat unrun from M1
    to M4 — not because no machine ran it, but because no command did. Never run
    the bare suite as a gate, for that reason.

    Deliberately NOT part of `composer verify` at the repo root: that chain has
    to stay runnable offline and without admin credentials, and wiring this in
    would quietly make `(cd console && composer install)` mandatory for everyone
    working on the POS app.
-->
<phpunit xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="vendor/phpunit/phpunit/phpunit.xsd"
         bootstrap="vendor/autoload.php"
         colors="true"
>
    <testsuites>
        <!-- Credential-free. Runs on a fresh clone. -->
        <testsuite name="Unit">
            <directory>tests/Unit</directory>
        </testsuite>
        <!-- Needs the live Supabase project and admin credentials. -->
        <testsuite name="Feature">
            <directory>tests/Feature</directory>
        </testsuite>
    </testsuites>
    <source>
        <include>
            <directory>app</directory>
        </include>
    </source>
    <php>
        <env name="APP_ENV" value="testing"/>
        <env name="CACHE_STORE" value="array"/>
        <env name="QUEUE_CONNECTION" value="sync"/>
        <env name="SESSION_DRIVER" value="array"/>
    </php>
</phpunit>
